.TH NP_SINK 3 "June 2026" "netpipe v0.1.0" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
np_sink_pcap, np_sink_pcapng, np_sink_json, np_sink_hex, np_sink_stats, np_sink_null, np_sink_pretty, np_sink_tuntap, np_sink_socket, np_sink_free, np_pipeline_add_sink \- netpipe packet output sinks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <netpipe.h>
.PP
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_pcap(const char *" path ");"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_pcapng(const char *" path ");"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_json(const char *" path ");"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_hex(const char *" path ");"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_stats(const char *" path ");"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_null(void);"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_pretty(const char *" path ");"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_tuntap(const char *" uri ");"
.br
.BI "np_sink_t *np_sink_socket(const char *" uri ");"
.br
.BI "void np_sink_free(np_sink_t *" s ");"
.br
.BI "np_err_t np_pipeline_add_sink(np_pipeline_t *" pl ", np_sink_t *" s ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions create, manage, and attach output sinks to a pipeline.
Sinks write, format, or transmit processed packets.
A single pipeline can have multiple sinks, causing packets to be duplicated to all outputs.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_pcap ()
function creates a binary PCAP sink that writes packets in the standard libpcap format to the file at
.IR path .
The output file is compatible with Wireshark and tcpdump.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_pcapng ()
function creates a PCAP-NG sink, allowing metadata such as interface descriptions and name resolution blocks to be saved.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_json ()
function creates a JSON formatter sink. It writes one JSON object per line (NDJSON) containing packet summaries and decoded layers to the file or stream specified by
.IR path .
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_hex ()
function creates an annotated hex dump sink that writes human-readable representations with protocol layer markers to
.IR path .
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_stats ()
function creates a statistics logger that periodically (every 5 seconds) dumps packet counters and protocol distributions to
.IR path .
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_null ()
function creates a null sink that discards all packet data. It is useful for benchmarking or when using rate-limiters/processors without requiring local output.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_pretty ()
function creates a tshark-style text logging sink.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_tuntap ()
function creates a virtual network interface injector sink.
The parameter
.I uri
should specify the tap device (e.g., "tun://tun0" or "tap://tap0"). Packets written to this sink are injected directly into the Linux kernel routing table.
Requires administrative privileges.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_socket ()
function creates a network forwarding sink.
The parameter
.I uri
should specify the target address (e.g., "socket://192.168.1.100:9999").
It opens a connection (TCP/UDP) and streams raw packets preceded by standard PCAP file headers.
.PP
The
.BR np_sink_free ()
function releases the resources allocated for the specified sink.
Sinks added to a pipeline via
.BR np_pipeline_add_sink ()
are automatically managed by the pipeline and will be freed when
.BR np_pipeline_free ()
is called; the caller must not free them.
.PP
The
.BR np_pipeline_add_sink ()
function attaches an output sink to the pipeline.
.SH RETURN VALUES
Sink creation functions return a pointer to a newly allocated
.B np_sink_t
on success, or
.B NULL
on failure.
.PP
.BR np_pipeline_add_sink ()
returns
.B NP_OK
on success, or a negative
.B np_err_t
code on failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libnetpipe (3),
.BR np_pipeline (3)
